<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/advanced/political-economy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Political economy studies <strong>the interlocking of power and wealth</strong> — how political
    institutions shape economic outcomes and how economic power shapes politics. After decades of
    being split between departments, the field reunited: you cannot explain inequality, regulation,
    trade or development with economics alone.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the interdependence, the varieties, the organised interests, the state" widget="equationStep" config={poleconCfg} />

  <h2>Questions the field owns</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who gets bailed out, and why?</strong> Financial crises: banks rescued, homeowners less often — the political-economy reading (concentrated, organised, informed financial interests vs diffuse publics) predicts the pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>The welfare state puzzle:</strong> why does the US spend less on welfare than Europe? Not just values — institutions (veto points, racialised politics, employer-based insurance lock-in) produce the divergence.</li>
    <li><strong>Globalisation's political limits:</strong> trade creates aggregate gains and concentrated losses (the economics lesson) — the political economy predicts and explains the backlash (the compensation that theory required rarely happened).</li>
    <li><strong>Power meets measurement:</strong> the field's toolkits — regression (the econometrics lesson) plus case comparison plus institutional history — triangulate because none alone survives the interdependence problem.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Olson, The Logic of Collective Action (1965); Hall & Soskice (2001); Rodrik, The Globalization Paradox (2011).</p>
</TopicPage>
